  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday morning through next Friday)\nIssued at 102 PM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nThe long term forecast remains a tale of two halves. On the front \nhalf will be above average temperatures for November as a relatively \nwarm and dry airmass creeps into the area on the beginning of the \nwork week. The back half will feature a system moving across the \neastern half the US that may bring some rain but seems highly likely \nto bring a cool down to more seasonable temperatures for mid-\nNovember.\n\nStarting on the front end Monday, the main polar jet should be to \nthe north of the CWA despite a strong upper level low rotating \nacross the NE. The air mass over the CWA will remain dry, but \nrepeated rounds of solar insolation each day should lead to warming \ninto the upper 60s to 70s for most by the late afternoon. This is a \ngood 5 to 10 degrees above average for daytime highs this time of \nyear. Flow remains to the north of the CWA through Wednesday with \nonly a cutoff low over Texas ejecting into the area but bringing no \nprecip with it. Forecast confidence is high through this time period.\n\nMoving into Thursday, forecast confidence begins to lower. Multiple \nwaves look to interact over the eastern coast, and the details of \nthose interactions including how and where they phase will have big \nimpacts on the forecast. EOF analysis of the 500 mb heights shows \nhighest variance along the western half of the trough digging into \nthe east coast, which isn't surprising when looking at some of the \nindividual members. Several shortwaves are embedded in the larger \nwave here, and the details of exactly where those will be this far \nout is a challenging model problem to say the least. K-mean cluster \nanalysis of the grand ensemble (US, Canada, and Euro) shows this \nchallenge beautifully, with each of the ensembles contributing \nmembers to each cluster. Some clusters are less phased (think 06Z \nGFS) on the western side, leading to greater chances of rainfall \nacross the southeast with a surface low that develops and taps into \nmoisture further to the south. The others show better phasing \nfurther east, which leads to less rainfall across the area. There \nhas been a trend towards the less phased solution across the \nensemble members, so the increase in PoPs for Thursday reflects \nthat.\n\nWhat is a bit more certain within the forecast on and after Thursday \nis that a cold front should sweep through the area, regardless of \nwhat the outcome of the above is. Highs on Friday will be in the 50s \nand 60s for most - much more seasonable. Lows will drop into the 30s \nby Friday night, with some areas across north Georgia seeing \nsubfreezing temperatures. Parts of the mountains and some of the \nvalleys are forecast to have a hard freeze with temperatures \ndropping into the mid 20s. The growing season has mostly wrapped up, \nbut those with vulnerable plants will need to get them indoors (or \nsheltered in some way).",
